  "result": "Only the actual delimiter line matches now, not the base64 `==` within the CSS. The fix resolves the issue.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe bug was in `src/generators/utils/parse.ts:146`. The delimiter regex `/\\s*={2,}\\s*/gm` matched any run of 2+ `=` characters anywhere in the text, including base64 padding (`==`) embedded in CSS `data:` URLs. This caused the parser to treat the trailing part of such lines (e.g. `') !important;`) as a new record/domain-pattern, which later broke `createUrlRegex` with an \"Unmatched `)`\" error.\n\nFix: anchor the delimiter regex to match only lines that consist entirely of `=` characters (optionally surrounded by whitespace), using `^\\s*={2,}\\s*$` with the multiline flag, so embedded `==` in base64 strings is no longer mistaken for a block delimiter.\n\nVerified: existing `tests/generators/utils/parse.tests.ts` suite (7 tests) still passes, and a manual check confirms the base64-padded CSS from the issue no longer matches the delimiter regex.",
